1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,160
Your phone doesn't start knowing where you

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,400
are. It starts with signal timings, and

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,320
satellite positions worked out from orbit

4
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,160
data the satellites broadcast.

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,400
From those, it finds you within about five

6
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,240
meters under open sky.

7
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,240
Let's see how, using nothing more than

8
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:16,880
circles, spheres and a little algebra.

9
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:19,360
Each GPS satellite broadcasts data

10
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,200
describing its orbit, so your phone can

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,280
calculate where the satellite was.

12
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,280
It also broadcasts the time the signal

13
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,400
left, kept by very precise atomic clocks.

14
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,400
Your phone compares that transmission time

15
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,960
with the arrival time on its own clock.

16
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,960
Multiply the travel time by the speed of

17
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,640
light, about three hundred thousand

18
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:38,480
kilometers per second, and you get an

19
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:39,600
apparent distance.

20
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,760
Engineers call it a pseudorange.

21
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:43,280
From twenty thousand two hundred

22
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,280
kilometers straight up, the travel time is

23
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,600
about seven hundredths of a second.

24
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:50,400
Let's start flat, on a map, with exact

25
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,360
distances. Say a station at point A tells

26
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,040
you that you're exactly five kilometers

27
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,230
away. That doesn't give you a point.

28
00:00:57,230 --> 00:00:59,310
It gives you a circle: every spot that's

29
00:00:59,310 --> 00:01:01,150
five kilometers from A.

30
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:03,790
Add a second station, B, eight kilometers

31
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:06,110
east of A. You're also five kilometers

32
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:08,590
from B. Now there are two circles, and

33
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:10,590
they cross at exactly two points.

34
00:01:10,590 --> 00:01:11,790
You're at one of them.

35
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:14,030
Which one? Take a second and guess.

36
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,870
Here's why there are two.

37
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:18,830
Put A at the origin, and B at eight on the

38
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:21,870
x axis. Write both circle equations, then

39
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:23,470
subtract one from the other.

40
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:26,030
The x squared and y squared terms cancel,

41
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:28,830
leaving sixteen x minus sixty four equals

42
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:31,870
zero. So x equals four: the crossing

43
00:01:31,870 --> 00:01:34,190
points sit exactly halfway between A and

44
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:37,310
B. Put x equals four back in, and y

45
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:38,510
squared equals nine.

46
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:41,230
A square has two roots, so y is plus three

47
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:42,750
or minus three.

48
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:45,950
Now add a third station, C, ten kilometers

49
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:48,190
south of the midpoint between A and B.

50
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:50,190
You're seven kilometers from C.

51
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:52,670
The southern point, four, minus three, is

52
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,830
exactly seven kilometers from C.

53
00:01:54,830 --> 00:01:57,390
The northern point is thirteen.

54
00:01:57,390 --> 00:01:59,230
Three circles, one point.

55
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,300
That's called trilateration.

56
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,020
Real satellites aren't on a flat map.

57
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:05,700
In three dimensions, circles become

58
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:08,500
spheres. Take the same idealized case,

59
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,260
with exact distances and a perfectly

60
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:11,700
synchronized clock.

61
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,620
One satellite puts you somewhere on a

62
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:16,340
sphere. Two spheres meet in a circle.

63
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:18,420
A third sphere can narrow that circle to

64
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:20,660
two candidate points, and the receiver can

65
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:22,260
often rule one out, because it isn't

66
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:23,700
anywhere plausible for someone near

67
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:24,980
Earth's surface.

68
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,380
So in that ideal case, three satellites

69
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:30,020
would do. But real phones don't have a

70
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,700
perfectly synchronized clock.

71
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,620
The satellites carry atomic clocks.

72
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:35,220
Your phone has an ordinary one.

73
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:37,380
If your phone's clock is one microsecond

74
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:39,700
ahead, every apparent distance comes out

75
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:41,620
about three hundred meters too long, in

76
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:43,220
this simplified model.

77
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,140
Here's what a shared error does on our

78
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:48,020
map. Let's exaggerate it, so every

79
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:50,180
apparent distance comes out one kilometer

80
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:52,740
too long: six, six and eight.

81
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:54,980
Now the three circles don't meet at a

82
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:55,940
single point.

83
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:58,820
But the error is the same for all three.

84
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:01,460
So try the same correction on every

85
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:04,190
distance. Subtract one kilometer, and

86
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:06,590
you're back to five, five and seven.

87
00:03:06,590 --> 00:03:08,830
Now all three circles pass through the

88
00:03:08,830 --> 00:03:11,470
same point: four, minus three.

89
00:03:11,470 --> 00:03:13,950
That correction is another unknown to

90
00:03:13,950 --> 00:03:16,350
solve for. The shrinking circles are just

91
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:17,470
a way to picture it.

92
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:19,870
Real receivers estimate position and clock

93
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:21,870
error together, and with noisy

94
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:23,550
measurements, the circles never meet

95
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:26,030
exactly. They find the best fit.

96
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:28,510
In three dimensions, a standalone fix has

97
00:03:28,510 --> 00:03:31,870
four unknowns: x, y, z, and the receiver's

98
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:33,150
clock offset.

99
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:35,390
So a receiver normally needs at least four

100
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:37,790
usable satellite signals, and it uses more

101
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:40,270
when it can. In a simplified model, each

102
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:42,510
one gives an equation: apparent distance

103
00:03:42,510 --> 00:03:44,910
equals the true geometric distance, plus

104
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:47,470
the speed of light times the clock offset.

105
00:03:47,470 --> 00:03:49,470
GPS satellites are arranged so that at

106
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:51,070
least four are visible from nearly

107
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:52,670
anywhere on Earth.

108
00:03:52,670 --> 00:03:54,670
So how close does it get?

109
00:03:54,670 --> 00:03:56,590
Under open sky, phones are typically

110
00:03:56,590 --> 00:03:58,350
accurate to about five meters.

111
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:00,590
Near buildings, bridges and trees it gets

112
00:04:00,590 --> 00:04:02,430
worse, because they block and reflect the

113
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:04,590
signals. With suitable equipment and

114
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:06,760
corrections, surveyors can get to within a

115
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:07,800
few centimeters.

116
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,520
And phones get help: Europe's Galileo adds

117
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:12,520
more satellite measurements, while Wi Fi

118
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:14,040
and cell signals help estimate your

119
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,440
location when satellite reception is poor.

120
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,760
So your phone never measures your position

121
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:21,240
directly. It measures timings, turns them

122
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:23,640
into apparent distances, and solves for

123
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:26,200
where it is and how far off its clock is.

124
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:28,360
On our flat map, one distance gave a

125
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:30,840
circle, two gave two points, and three

126
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:33,800
gave one. In space, the fourth satellite

127
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:35,800
provides the extra measurement needed to

128
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:37,880
solve for position and clock error

129
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:38,760
together.

130
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:40,600
If you liked this video, please give it a

131
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:42,520
like and subscribe for more everyday

132
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:44,120
engineering. Thanks for watching!
